# cine act=fall fx="code:@two_accounts;l:wework_interior_nyc_fulton_elevator;r:wework_neumann_2015_disrupt_portrait;lt:THE COMPANY · WEWORK;rt:THE FOUNDER · ADAM NEUMANN;open:no;win:r;wat:split;crack:split;lv:KEEPS THE LEASES;lat:split;rv:SELLS HIS SHARES;rat:money;in:right;out:right"
{grave} And that's when the two accounts **split**, and the money went one way.

// CARD a4-02 · SEE: SoftBank's real release behind, dim; $9.5B sets as the headline over an empty dashed bar; on "five" the gold $5B part fills from the left, on "six" the $1.5B part joins it: FOR THE COMPANY — the last third of the bar stays empty · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@headline_split;bg:wework_doc_softbank_release_body_p2;head:$9.5 BILLION^THE HEADLINE · OCTOBER 2019;total:9.5;parts:$5B^NEW FINANCING^rescued~$1.5B^EARLIER COMMITMENT, SOONER^six;in:right"
In October, SoftBank rescued WeWork. The headline said nine and a half billion **dollars**.
But only six and a half billion was for the **company**.

// CARD a4-03 · SEE: SoftBank's real release on the left; the gold $6.5B arrow into WeWork is drawn on "was"; on "offer" a blue arrow forks to Neumann himself: $3B TO BUY EXISTING SHARES, his among them · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@money_fork;src:wework_doc_softbank_release_cover_p1;srclab:SOFTBANK · OCT 2019;a:wework_bldg_denver_vault^INTO WEWORK^$6.5B^was;b:wework_neumann_2015_disrupt_close^SHAREHOLDERS, NEUMANN AMONG THEM^$3B^offer"
Three billion was an offer to buy shares from existing **owners**.
One of them was the founder, Adam **Neumann**.

// CARD a4-05 · SEE: the WSJ page of 22 October 2019 behind, dim; $1.7B sets as the headline over a dashed bar; on "nine" the $970M SHARES part fills the bar from the left · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@headline_split;bg:wework_doc_news_wsj_softbank_control_2019_10_22;head:$1.7 BILLION^HIS EXIT, AS REPORTED;total:1700;parts:$970M^FOR HIS SHARES^nine;out:right"
His exit was reported as one point seven billion **dollars**.
In fact, about nine hundred and seventy million was for his **shares**.

// CARD a4-06 · SEE: three solid blocks on a dark floor: SHARES $970M already standing; on "Five" a hatched block rises, $500M CREDIT LINE (A LOAN); on "consulting" a small one, $185M FEE · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@blocks;vals:.97,.5,.185;labs:SHARES,CREDIT LINE · A LOAN,CONSULTING FEE;txts:$970M,$500M,$185M;show:1;ats:Five,consulting;bg:wework_clip_ipo_signing_5;in:right"
Five hundred million was a credit line, which is a **loan**.
And one hundred and eighty-five million was a consulting **fee**.

// CARD a4-07 · SEE: the WSJ's page of 22 October 2019 under the lamp; on "cancelled" a red line strikes its $1.7B line; on "disclosed" black redaction bars sweep across the rest · SEAM out right · HOLD
# cine act=after fx="code:@redact_bars;doc:wework_doc_news_wsj_softbank_control_2019_10_22;crop:.03,.12,.97,.75;strike:.25,.665,.53,.695;sat:cancelled;go:disclosed;out:right"
Part of that deal was later cancelled, then settled on terms that were never fully **disclosed**.

// CARD a4-08 · SEE: a rusted gate chained shut with a yellow padlock: the company locked in with its leases · SEAM in right, HOLD
# cine act=after fx="code:@reel;clip:stock_prison_gate_03;move:in;z:.06;in:right"
{sad} The company kept the **leases**.

