Then, in twenty twenty, the pandemic emptied the **offices** in every city.

// CARD a4-10 · SEE: a dim empty office behind; the split-flap calendar runs MAR 2020 → NOV 2023 on "didn't" and locks; STILL OPEN sets under it · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@month_flip;from:2020-03;to:2023-11;go:kill;lab:STILL OPEN;sub:the pandemic in 2020 → the bankruptcy filing in November 2023;bg:wework_clip_empty_office_02;in:right"
But it didn't kill WeWork. WeWork lasted three more **years**.

// CARD a4-12 · SEE: over the dim exchange: an empty box already stands on THE STOCK MARKET line (LISTED SHELL); on "merger" a gold WEWORK card slides into it and the lid closes; THE USUAL FILING card above is struck through on "skips" · SEAM in right · HOLD
# cine act=rise fx="code:@shell_merge;bg:wework_ctx_wallstreet_looking_down;shell:LISTED SHELL (BOWX);co:WEWORK;go:merger;skip:skips;in:right"
In October twenty twenty-one, it finally reached the stock market, through a **SPAC**.
A SPAC is a shell company that's already on the stock market, so a merger skips the usual **filing**.

// CARD a4-13 · SEE: three blocks on a dark floor: $9B ENTERPRISE VALUE rises on "nine", $6.55B EQUITY VALUE on "six", a short $1.28B CASH RAISED on "one" — each smaller than the last · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@blocks;vals:9,6.553,1.283;labs:ENTERPRISE VALUE,EQUITY VALUE,CASH RAISED (GROSS);txts:$9B,$6.55B,$1.28B;ats:nine,six,one;bg:stock_finance_wall_street_sign_04;hl:2;hlat:raised;note:the SPAC, October 2021 (SEC filings);out:right"
The headline value was nine billion **dollars**.
But the shares were valued at about six and a half **billion**.
And the cash it actually raised was about one point three **billion**.

